<section>
        <section>
            <h2>Are HTML tags used correctly to give structure to the content? </h2>
        </section>

        <section >
            <h3>Headings</h3>
            <p>Look at the page and identify the various sections and their headings, then check if the code is consistent with the visual structure</p>

            <ul>
                <li>Are all headings marked up as headings? (WCAG 1.3.1)</li>
                <li>Are only headings marked up as headings? (WCAG 1.3.1)</li>
                <li>Are heading levels correct? (WCAG 1.3.1)</li>
                <li>Are headings contents meaningful? (WCAG 2.4.6) </li>
            </ul>

        </section>

        <section>
            <h3>Example : visual structure</h3>
            <p><img src="https://images.anysurfer.be/structure/structure_visuelle.png" alt="indication of visual heading structure on AnySurfer training page"></p>
        </section>


        <section>
            <h3>How to test ? </h3>
            <p>Choose one of the methods below : </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Web developer toolbar: Outline Headings (Check 'Show Element Tag Names')</li>
                <li>Outline headings bookmarklet</li>
                <li>a11y outline extension in Firefox</li>
                <li>Read page with a screenreader</li>
                <li>Inspect code with dev tools in case of doubt</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section>
            <h3>Example : highlight headings with bookmarklet </h3>
            <p><img src="https://images.anysurfer.be/structure/structure_code.png" alt="indication of heading tags on AnySurfer blog about WCAG 2.2"></p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h3>Example : outline headings with web developer extension</h3>
            <p><img src="https://images.anysurfer.be/gijs/20200624-15-44-31.jpg" alt="screenshot outline headings on a page of AnySurfer website "></p>
        </section>
        <section>
            <h3>Example : show heading structure with a11Y outline bookmarklet in Firefox</h3>
            <p><img src="https://images.anysurfer.be/structure/a11y_outline_firefox.png" alt="heading structure of AnySurfer blog about WCAG 2.2"></p>
        </section>


        <section>
            <h3>Headings: points of attention</h3> 
            <ul>
                <li>Not all big or bold text is a heading</li>
                <li>A heading should generally be followed by related content. If there is no content after a heading it probably shouldn't be announced as a heading.</li>
                <li>It is OK to skip heading levels but the hierarchy (nesting) must remain logical.</li>
            </ul>
        <p>More info about <a target="tweede" href="https://www.tpgi.com/heading-off-confusion-when-do-headings-fail-wcag/">When do headings fail WCAG?</a></p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h3>Example : a bold text is not always a heading</h3> 
                <p><img src="https://images.anysurfer.be/structure/intro_not_heading.png" alt="4 lines of bold introduction text is not a heading"></p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h3>Lists</h3>
            <p>Look at the page and identify groups of elements that should be structured as lists</p>

            <ul>
                <li>Links in a navigation menu</li>
                <li>unordered lists (bullets) in the content</li>
                <li>ordered lists in the content (numbered)</li>
                <li>lists of links or other realted elements</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Make sure they are correctly codes as lists</p>
        </section>
        <section class="compact">
            <h3>Attention points for lists</h3>

            <ul>
                <li>Only one ul or ol element containing the whole list
                <pre><code is:raw>&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;First list element&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Second list element&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Third list element&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre></li>
                <li>Proper nesting 
                <pre><code is:raw>&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;First list element
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;nested list 1st element&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;nested list 2nd element&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Second list element&lt;/li&gt;
    ...
&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre></li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h3>Landmarks</h3>
            <p>Are the pages structured by landmark regions?  </p>
            <ul>
                <li>a header (announced as banner)</li>
                <li>a navigation region (announced as navigation), usually in the header</li>
                <li>a main region (announced as main) </li>
                <li>a footer (announced as content info)</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Test with "highlight landmarks" bookmarklet</p>
        </section>

        <section>
                    <p><img src="https://images.anysurfer.be/structure/landmarks_anysurfer.png" alt="highlighted landmarks on AnySurfer website"></p>

        </section>


        <section>
            <h3>Tables</h3>
            <p>Inspect with web developer extension : outline tables cells </p>
            <ul>

                <li>Are the table headings  correctly marked up (th) ?</li>
                <li>Are there  no empty rows ? </li>
                <li><a target="tweede" href="https://examples.anysurfer.be/2.4.2/table_to_be_corrected.html">Incorrect table</a></li>
                <li><a target="tweede" href="https://examples.anysurfer.be/2.4.2/table_corrected.html">Correct table</a></li>

            </ul>
        </section>

